  "worked_solution": "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\u0394{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $\u0394x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda \u0394x)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda \u0394x| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda \u0394x<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda \u0394x<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $\u0394x=\\frac{0.25}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda \u0394x=0.25.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda \u0394x & <2 \\\\ 0<0.25 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $\u0394x=\\frac{3.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda \u0394x=3.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $\u0394x=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda \u0394x=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $\u0394x=\\frac{1.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda \u0394x=1.5.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda \u0394x & <2 \\\\ 0<1.5 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\".",
